On the Raspberry Pi device (here, I used the Raspberry Pi 4 model B running the default Raspberry
Pi OS), we log in and get ourselves a root shell via a simple sudo -s. We then build the kernel module.
If you have installed the default Raspberry Pi image on the device, all required development tools,
kernel headers, and more will be pre-installed! So, log in to the board, build the module, and try it out.
